deepDives.body:
Chunking is where most quality is won or lost, so treat each format on its own terms and converge on one chunk shape.

**Target chunk shape:**

```ts
type Chunk = {
  id: string;            // sha1(docId + headingPath + ordinal)
  docId: string;
  version: number;
  headingPath: string[]; // ["Billing", "Refunds", "Annual plans"]
  text: string;          // what the LLM sees
  embedText: string;     // title + heading path + text (what gets embedded)
  page?: number;         // PDFs, for citations
  url: string;           // with #anchor where possible
  tokens: number;
  hash: string;
};
```

**Markdown:** parse to an AST (remark), walk headings to build the path, start a new chunk at each H2/H3, then split oversized sections recursively by paragraph, never inside a fenced code block or a table. Merge tiny sections (under ~80 tokens) into their neighbour so you do not index orphan headings.

**PDF:** use a layout-aware extractor, remove repeated headers and footers (lines that recur on most pages), detect headings by font size or the PDF outline, keep page numbers. Tables become Markdown with the header row repeated per chunk. Scanned pages go through OCR.

**Web pages:** fetch with a size and time cap, extract the main content (Readability-style), drop nav and footers, use the page's headings as structure, keep the canonical URL.

**Size:** target ~400 tokens, hard cap below the embedding model's limit, ~15% overlap only when a section is split mid-way. Measure: run the golden set with 250, 400 and 800 token targets and record recall@5 and faithfulness; put the table in your README. That table is exactly what interviewers want to see.

The UI should show tokens within about a second and sources immediately, without letting the model invent citations.

1. Retrieval finishes, the server sends a `sources` event first: `[{ id: "S1", title, url, snippet }]`. The UI renders source cards right away.
2. The LLM streams text containing markers like `[S2]`. The client parses markers incrementally and turns them into links to the matching card; unknown ids are rendered as plain text and logged.
3. When a sentence completes, the server checks support (a fast NLI model or a small LLM judge on sentence plus cited passage). It sends a `verdict` event; unsupported sentences get a subtle "unverified" style. Cap the number of checks per answer for cost.
4. On finish, persist the trace: question, rewritten query, chunk ids and scores, prompt version, model, tokens, latency per stage, answer, citation verdicts.

```ts
for await (const part of stream) {
  if (part.type === "text-delta") buffer += part.text;
  for (const sentence of takeCompleteSentences(buffer)) queueVerify(sentence, sources);
}
```

Use your framework's streaming primitives (for example the AI SDK's UI message stream, or server-sent events) and render the answer as markdown without raw HTML, with links restricted to your own source URLs: this also blocks exfiltration through injected links.
